WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.551
Hi, I'm Noodle!

00:00:02.551 --> 00:00:06.754
RLCD means reinforcement learning for calibrated decisions, not constrained decoding.

00:00:06.754 --> 00:00:12.217
It's how TypeSafe says it trains Jev, a model that never writes text.

00:00:13.683 --> 00:00:17.712
The contract: the model returns a decision and a probability, never text.

00:00:17.712 --> 00:00:22.077
Answers given point eight should be right about eighty percent of the time.

00:00:23.675 --> 00:00:27.354
The caller supplies the options at request time, up to two hundred

00:00:27.354 --> 00:00:30.420
and fifty-five, and the model has never seen them before.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.764
In an open reproduction, a small head reads the question and one

00:00:34.764 --> 00:00:37.053
option together, and outputs a single number.

00:00:37.053 --> 00:00:38.034
Once per option.

00:00:38.454 --> 00:00:42.754
A softmax across that question's options turns those numbers into probabilities.

00:00:42.754 --> 00:00:47.053
Jev probably works this way too, though its head is undisclosed.

00:00:48.608 --> 00:00:52.309
Score a report by its plain distance from the outcome, and a

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:55.394
model that believes seventy percent does best by claiming certainty.

00:00:55.814 --> 00:01:00.112
A proper scoring rule, like cross-entropy or Brier, is minimised by

00:01:00.112 --> 00:01:01.676
reporting your true belief.

00:01:01.676 --> 00:01:04.802
The open reproductions train on both, added together.

00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:10.597
Pretrained models are already well calibrated when choosing among options.

00:01:10.597 --> 00:01:14.764
Preference tuning made GPT-4's calibration error ten times worse.

00:01:14.764 --> 00:01:18.931
TypeSafe publishes no method or calibration number at all.

00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:24.234
Calibration probably comes less from a new algorithm than from a proper

00:01:24.234 --> 00:01:26.897
scoring rule, and from not doing what breaks it.

00:01:29.775 --> 00:01:33.873
To recap: decisions, not text; one score per option; and a loss

00:01:33.873 --> 00:01:36.605
that makes the honest probability the cheapest one.

00:01:37.025 --> 00:01:39.074
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.074 --> 00:01:39.660
I'm Noodle.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:39.953
Bye!
